1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,159
Every time Concorde landed,

2
00:00:02,159 --> 00:00:04,159
its whole nose bent down,

3
00:00:04,159 --> 00:00:06,559
like a bird lowering its beak.

4
00:00:06,739 --> 00:00:08,213
It looked strange,

5
00:00:08,213 --> 00:00:10,834
almost alive, and it was not for

6
00:00:10,834 --> 00:00:11,244
show.

7
00:00:11,424 --> 00:00:12,632
Without that bend,

8
00:00:12,632 --> 00:00:14,913
the pilots could not have seen the

9
00:00:14,913 --> 00:00:15,383
runway.

10
00:00:15,563 --> 00:00:18,340
Concorde was full of parts like that,

11
00:00:18,340 --> 00:00:20,741
a glass shield over the windows,

12
00:00:20,741 --> 00:00:22,467
a wheel under the tail,

13
00:00:22,467 --> 00:00:24,944
fuel that moved around in flight.

14
00:00:25,124 --> 00:00:28,688
Today, ten parts of the fastest airliner

15
00:00:28,688 --> 00:00:32,610
ever to carry passengers in regular service,

16
00:00:32,610 --> 00:00:34,748
and why each one exists.

17
00:00:35,099 --> 00:00:38,188
Concorde first flew on the second of

18
00:00:38,188 --> 00:00:40,419
March nineteen sixty-nine,

19
00:00:40,419 --> 00:00:44,538
entered airline service in nineteen seventy-six,

20
00:00:44,538 --> 00:00:47,799
and flew passengers at twice the speed

21
00:00:47,799 --> 00:00:51,146
of sound, at about sixty thousand feet,

22
00:00:51,146 --> 00:00:53,291
until two thousand three.

23
00:00:53,471 --> 00:00:54,676
At that speed,

24
00:00:54,676 --> 00:00:59,149
almost every normal airliner solution stops working.

25
00:00:59,329 --> 00:01:00,341
Air gets hot,

26
00:01:00,341 --> 00:01:01,820
the airplane grows,

27
00:01:01,820 --> 00:01:04,623
and even the windows have to change.

28
00:01:04,803 --> 00:01:07,839
So the engineers had to invent new

29
00:01:07,839 --> 00:01:08,286
ones.

30
00:01:08,636 --> 00:01:11,730
As always, every fact here comes from

31
00:01:11,730 --> 00:01:12,985
a named source,

32
00:01:12,985 --> 00:01:17,167
museums, aviation references and official reports,

33
00:01:17,167 --> 00:01:19,677
all listed in the description.

34
00:01:19,857 --> 00:01:22,402
Let's start with the most famous part

35
00:01:22,402 --> 00:01:23,572
of all, the nose.

36
00:01:23,921 --> 00:01:26,824
Why did Concorde's nose bend down?

37
00:01:27,004 --> 00:01:28,699
Because of its wing.

38
00:01:28,879 --> 00:01:31,622
A delta wing like Concorde's has no

39
00:01:31,622 --> 00:01:33,973
flaps, so to fly slowly it has

40
00:01:33,973 --> 00:01:36,402
to point its nose high into the

41
00:01:36,402 --> 00:01:36,716
air.

42
00:01:36,896 --> 00:01:39,703
On landing, Concorde came in with its

43
00:01:39,703 --> 00:01:41,069
nose so high that,

44
00:01:41,069 --> 00:01:42,663
with a straight nose,

45
00:01:42,663 --> 00:01:45,394
the pilots would have seen only sky.

46
00:01:45,574 --> 00:01:47,583
So the whole nose was hinged.

47
00:01:47,763 --> 00:01:50,546
It drooped to about five degrees for

48
00:01:50,546 --> 00:01:51,861
takeoff and taxi,

49
00:01:51,861 --> 00:01:54,412
and twelve and a half degrees for

50
00:01:54,412 --> 00:01:55,031
landing.

51
00:01:55,380 --> 00:01:58,201
Why not just give Concorde a short,

52
00:01:58,201 --> 00:02:00,942
round nose like a normal airliner?

53
00:02:01,122 --> 00:02:04,036
Because at twice the speed of sound,

54
00:02:04,036 --> 00:02:06,627
a blunt nose makes a huge amount

55
00:02:06,627 --> 00:02:07,275
of drag.

56
00:02:07,455 --> 00:02:10,684
A supersonic airplane needs a long,

57
00:02:10,684 --> 00:02:12,529
sharp, pointed nose.

58
00:02:12,709 --> 00:02:14,742
That is perfect at Mach two,

59
00:02:14,742 --> 00:02:17,283
and terrible for seeing the runway.

60
00:02:17,463 --> 00:02:20,289
The droop nose was the compromise,

61
00:02:20,289 --> 00:02:21,703
sharp in the sky,

62
00:02:21,703 --> 00:02:24,197
and bent down near the ground.

63
00:02:24,546 --> 00:02:27,037
The nose moved with hydraulic power,

64
00:02:27,037 --> 00:02:29,597
controlled by a lever in the cockpit.

65
00:02:29,777 --> 00:02:32,412
And the engineers planned for failure.

66
00:02:32,592 --> 00:02:35,725
If the hydraulics or the electrics failed,

67
00:02:35,725 --> 00:02:38,409
the locks could be released by hand,

68
00:02:38,409 --> 00:02:40,721
and the nose would fall to five

69
00:02:40,721 --> 00:02:43,332
degrees by gravity and the airflow.

70
00:02:43,512 --> 00:02:46,159
A part this important could never be

71
00:02:46,159 --> 00:02:48,585
allowed to get stuck in the wrong

72
00:02:48,585 --> 00:02:49,247
position.

73
00:02:49,597 --> 00:02:52,438
Why did Concorde have a second windshield

74
00:02:52,438 --> 00:02:54,240
in front of the first one?

75
00:02:54,420 --> 00:02:56,394
It is called the visor.

76
00:02:56,574 --> 00:02:59,096
In cruise, it slid up over the

77
00:02:59,096 --> 00:03:02,207
real windshield and made the nose one

78
00:03:02,207 --> 00:03:03,888
smooth, sharp shape.

79
00:03:04,068 --> 00:03:06,906
It protected the windshield from heat and

80
00:03:06,906 --> 00:03:08,636
air pressure at Mach two,

81
00:03:08,636 --> 00:03:11,058
and it made the cockpit very quiet.

82
00:03:11,238 --> 00:03:13,836
Pilots said that the first time they

83
00:03:13,836 --> 00:03:16,651
raised it, they thought the engines had

84
00:03:16,651 --> 00:03:17,228
stopped.

85
00:03:17,579 --> 00:03:20,532
The first prototypes had a metal visor

86
00:03:20,532 --> 00:03:23,175
with tiny windows and a periscope.

87
00:03:23,355 --> 00:03:25,903
One test pilot said that flying it

88
00:03:25,903 --> 00:03:28,602
was like flying inside a letter box.

89
00:03:28,782 --> 00:03:33,379
The American Federal Aviation Administration wanted better

90
00:03:33,379 --> 00:03:36,866
forward vision, so the engineers developed a

91
00:03:36,866 --> 00:03:39,957
glass visor that could survive the heat

92
00:03:39,957 --> 00:03:42,018
and the force of Mach two.

93
00:03:42,198 --> 00:03:44,117
It was about one and a half

94
00:03:44,117 --> 00:03:46,748
inches thick, with a heated gold film

95
00:03:46,748 --> 00:03:47,956
to keep it clear.

96
00:03:48,306 --> 00:03:50,849
There was a clever safety lock too.

97
00:03:51,029 --> 00:03:52,438
With the visor up,

98
00:03:52,438 --> 00:03:55,568
the landing gear circuits were isolated,

99
00:03:55,568 --> 00:03:58,150
so nobody could lower the gear at

100
00:03:58,150 --> 00:04:00,967
twice the speed of sound by mistake.

101
00:04:01,147 --> 00:04:04,330
Visor down and nose down meant slow

102
00:04:04,330 --> 00:04:04,967
flight.

103
00:04:05,147 --> 00:04:07,718
Visor up and nose up meant Mach

104
00:04:07,718 --> 00:04:08,049
two.

105
00:04:08,229 --> 00:04:09,928
Just by looking at the nose,

106
00:04:09,928 --> 00:04:12,293
you could tell what Concorde was doing.

107
00:04:12,642 --> 00:04:15,928
Why did Concorde's wing have no flaps?

108
00:04:16,108 --> 00:04:19,236
Normal airliners have flaps and slats that

109
00:04:19,236 --> 00:04:21,693
slide out to make the wing bigger

110
00:04:21,693 --> 00:04:22,586
for landing.

111
00:04:22,766 --> 00:04:25,588
Concorde's thin delta wing had none.

112
00:04:25,768 --> 00:04:27,768
Instead, at high angles,

113
00:04:27,768 --> 00:04:30,435
the sharp front edge of the wing

114
00:04:30,435 --> 00:04:33,852
creates two huge spinning vortices of air

115
00:04:33,852 --> 00:04:35,102
above the wing.

116
00:04:35,282 --> 00:04:37,802
Those swirls create low pressure,

117
00:04:37,802 --> 00:04:40,552
and that low pressure keeps the wing

118
00:04:40,552 --> 00:04:42,155
lifting at low speed.

119
00:04:42,505 --> 00:04:44,597
This is called vortex lift,

120
00:04:44,597 --> 00:04:46,766
and on a humid day you could

121
00:04:46,766 --> 00:04:48,005
actually see it,

122
00:04:48,005 --> 00:04:51,103
as white swirls of condensation over the

123
00:04:51,103 --> 00:04:53,349
wings on takeoff and landing.

124
00:04:53,529 --> 00:04:55,944
The wing was not a simple triangle.

125
00:04:56,124 --> 00:04:57,836
It had a gentle curve,

126
00:04:57,836 --> 00:04:59,471
called an ogee shape,

127
00:04:59,471 --> 00:05:02,662
refined over about five thousand hours of

128
00:05:02,662 --> 00:05:04,218
wind tunnel testing,

129
00:05:04,218 --> 00:05:06,553
so that one wing could work at

130
00:05:06,553 --> 00:05:09,043
two hundred miles an hour and at

131
00:05:09,043 --> 00:05:11,612
one thousand three hundred fifty.

132
00:05:11,961 --> 00:05:14,642
The price of vortex lift is that

133
00:05:14,642 --> 00:05:16,234
high nose up angle.

134
00:05:16,414 --> 00:05:18,589
And that is the link between all

135
00:05:18,589 --> 00:05:20,628
the first parts of this video.

136
00:05:20,808 --> 00:05:23,080
The wing needs a high angle,

137
00:05:23,080 --> 00:05:25,108
so the nose has to droop,

138
00:05:25,108 --> 00:05:27,136
so the visor has to move.

139
00:05:27,316 --> 00:05:30,822
One design decision created three moving parts.

140
00:05:31,002 --> 00:05:33,220
That is how airplanes are built.

141
00:05:33,400 --> 00:05:36,279
Every solution creates the next problem.

142
00:05:36,628 --> 00:05:38,901
Why did Concorde have a small set

143
00:05:38,901 --> 00:05:40,622
of wheels under its tail?

144
00:05:40,802 --> 00:05:43,055
Because of that high angle again.

145
00:05:43,235 --> 00:05:46,316
Concorde lifted off with its nose pointing

146
00:05:46,316 --> 00:05:48,224
about eighteen degrees up,

147
00:05:48,224 --> 00:05:50,572
far more than a normal airliner.

148
00:05:50,752 --> 00:05:52,271
With such a long body,

149
00:05:52,271 --> 00:05:54,411
a pilot pulling up a little too

150
00:05:54,411 --> 00:05:56,689
much could scrape the tail on the

151
00:05:56,689 --> 00:05:57,172
runway.

152
00:05:57,352 --> 00:05:59,704
The tail wheels were there to touch

153
00:05:59,704 --> 00:06:01,787
first and protect the airplane.

154
00:06:02,137 --> 00:06:03,752
To reach that angle,

155
00:06:03,752 --> 00:06:07,302
Concorde also needed very tall landing gear,

156
00:06:07,302 --> 00:06:09,724
so the main legs were long and

157
00:06:09,724 --> 00:06:12,064
had to fold into a thin wing.

158
00:06:12,244 --> 00:06:15,558
The legs even shortened as they retracted,

159
00:06:15,558 --> 00:06:18,084
to fit into the space available.

160
00:06:18,264 --> 00:06:21,459
Takeoff speed was about two hundred fifty

161
00:06:21,459 --> 00:06:22,550
miles an hour,

162
00:06:22,550 --> 00:06:26,368
and landing speed about one hundred eighty-seven,

163
00:06:26,368 --> 00:06:29,096
much faster than a normal airliner.

164
00:06:29,445 --> 00:06:31,718
A tail bumper sounds like a small

165
00:06:31,718 --> 00:06:34,405
detail, but a tailstrike can damage the

166
00:06:34,405 --> 00:06:37,298
pressure bulkhead and the structure at the

167
00:06:37,298 --> 00:06:38,675
back of an airliner.

168
00:06:38,855 --> 00:06:41,631
On Concorde, with its long thin body

169
00:06:41,631 --> 00:06:42,942
and steep angles,

170
00:06:42,942 --> 00:06:45,950
the engineers decided it was simpler to

171
00:06:45,950 --> 00:06:48,495
give the tail its own wheels than

172
00:06:48,495 --> 00:06:51,348
to trust every takeoff to be perfect.

173
00:06:51,697 --> 00:06:55,439
Why were Concorde's engine intakes so long

174
00:06:55,439 --> 00:06:56,864
and rectangular?

175
00:06:57,044 --> 00:07:00,200
Because a jet engine cannot swallow air

176
00:07:00,200 --> 00:07:02,466
at twice the speed of sound.

177
00:07:02,646 --> 00:07:04,823
The air must be slowed down to

178
00:07:04,823 --> 00:07:07,290
below the speed of sound before it

179
00:07:07,290 --> 00:07:08,451
reaches the fan.

180
00:07:08,631 --> 00:07:11,928
Inside each intake there were movable ramps,

181
00:07:11,928 --> 00:07:14,925
which created shock waves in exactly the

182
00:07:14,925 --> 00:07:17,772
right places, slowing the air down and

183
00:07:17,772 --> 00:07:20,020
squeezing it at the same time.

184
00:07:20,370 --> 00:07:22,662
The ramps moved all the time,

185
00:07:22,662 --> 00:07:24,164
depending on speed,

186
00:07:24,164 --> 00:07:26,139
altitude and temperature,

187
00:07:26,139 --> 00:07:29,063
and they were controlled by a digital

188
00:07:29,063 --> 00:07:29,775
computer.

189
00:07:29,955 --> 00:07:32,519
According to aviation historians,

190
00:07:32,519 --> 00:07:34,928
it was the first time a digital

191
00:07:34,928 --> 00:07:38,424
computer had full authority over an essential

192
00:07:38,424 --> 00:07:40,833
system on a passenger airplane.

193
00:07:41,013 --> 00:07:41,924
At Mach two,

194
00:07:41,924 --> 00:07:44,429
the intake was doing a huge share

195
00:07:44,429 --> 00:07:47,085
of the work of compressing the air.

196
00:07:47,435 --> 00:07:50,061
If an engine failed at Mach two,

197
00:07:50,061 --> 00:07:52,850
the intake had to react instantly,

198
00:07:52,850 --> 00:07:55,804
or the shock waves could disturb the

199
00:07:55,804 --> 00:07:57,281
engine next to it.

200
00:07:57,461 --> 00:08:00,224
So spill doors opened and the ramps

201
00:08:00,224 --> 00:08:02,987
moved fully to protect the airflow.

202
00:08:03,167 --> 00:08:06,039
On Concorde, the engine was not just

203
00:08:06,039 --> 00:08:08,113
the part that burned fuel.

204
00:08:08,293 --> 00:08:10,350
The whole channel in front of it

205
00:08:10,350 --> 00:08:11,892
was part of the machine.

206
00:08:12,241 --> 00:08:15,479
Why did a passenger airliner have afterburners

207
00:08:15,479 --> 00:08:16,816
like a fighter jet?

208
00:08:16,996 --> 00:08:20,309
Afterburners spray extra fuel into the hot

209
00:08:20,309 --> 00:08:21,887
exhaust and burn it,

210
00:08:21,887 --> 00:08:23,939
for a big boost of thrust.

211
00:08:24,119 --> 00:08:28,283
Concorde's four Rolls-Royce Olympus engines used them

212
00:08:28,283 --> 00:08:30,640
only twice in a normal flight,

213
00:08:30,640 --> 00:08:33,469
for takeoff, and to push through the

214
00:08:33,469 --> 00:08:36,926
sound barrier, between about Mach zero point

215
00:08:36,926 --> 00:08:39,676
nine five and Mach one point seven.

216
00:08:40,025 --> 00:08:42,432
The sound barrier is where drag rises

217
00:08:42,432 --> 00:08:45,033
the most, and Concorde needed extra push

218
00:08:45,033 --> 00:08:46,724
to get through it quickly.

219
00:08:46,904 --> 00:08:49,229
Once above Mach one point seven,

220
00:08:49,229 --> 00:08:51,772
the afterburners were switched off,

221
00:08:51,772 --> 00:08:54,606
and Concorde cruised at twice the speed

222
00:08:54,606 --> 00:08:56,204
of sound without them.

223
00:08:56,384 --> 00:08:58,627
That is called supercruise,

224
00:08:58,627 --> 00:09:02,282
something very few airplanes have ever done,

225
00:09:02,282 --> 00:09:03,861
even military ones.

226
00:09:04,210 --> 00:09:06,834
Afterburners burn fuel very fast,

227
00:09:06,834 --> 00:09:09,457
and Concorde was already thirsty,

228
00:09:09,457 --> 00:09:12,716
using more than five thousand six hundred

229
00:09:12,716 --> 00:09:14,067
gallons per hour.

230
00:09:14,247 --> 00:09:15,187
That is why,

231
00:09:15,187 --> 00:09:16,830
from London or Paris,

232
00:09:16,830 --> 00:09:19,492
it could cross the Atlantic to New

233
00:09:19,492 --> 00:09:21,527
York, but not the Pacific.

234
00:09:21,707 --> 00:09:24,869
The fastest east to west crossing took

235
00:09:24,869 --> 00:09:27,198
three hours and six minutes.

236
00:09:27,548 --> 00:09:30,264
Why did Concorde pump its fuel from

237
00:09:30,264 --> 00:09:32,591
one end of the airplane to the

238
00:09:32,591 --> 00:09:34,143
other during flight?

239
00:09:34,323 --> 00:09:37,367
Because when an airplane goes supersonic,

240
00:09:37,367 --> 00:09:40,040
the point where the wing's lift acts

241
00:09:40,040 --> 00:09:42,861
moves backward, by about two meters on

242
00:09:42,861 --> 00:09:43,530
Concorde.

243
00:09:43,710 --> 00:09:45,584
That would make the nose want to

244
00:09:45,584 --> 00:09:46,229
pitch down.

245
00:09:46,409 --> 00:09:48,748
Normally you fix that with the tail

246
00:09:48,748 --> 00:09:51,487
controls, but on Concorde that would have

247
00:09:51,487 --> 00:09:52,957
created too much drag.

248
00:09:53,306 --> 00:09:56,014
So the flight engineer moved the weight

249
00:09:56,014 --> 00:09:56,569
instead.

250
00:09:56,749 --> 00:10:00,287
During acceleration, fuel was pumped toward the

251
00:10:00,287 --> 00:10:01,868
back of the airplane,

252
00:10:01,868 --> 00:10:04,879
moving the center of gravity backward to

253
00:10:04,879 --> 00:10:06,083
follow the lift.

254
00:10:06,263 --> 00:10:08,787
When slowing down before landing,

255
00:10:08,787 --> 00:10:11,081
fuel was pumped forward again.

256
00:10:11,261 --> 00:10:13,381
The fuel was not only energy for

257
00:10:13,381 --> 00:10:14,176
the engines.

258
00:10:14,356 --> 00:10:16,467
It was also a moving weight that

259
00:10:16,467 --> 00:10:18,315
balanced the whole airplane.

260
00:10:18,664 --> 00:10:21,439
This trick is now used on other

261
00:10:21,439 --> 00:10:23,676
long range airliners too,

262
00:10:23,676 --> 00:10:26,182
with trim tanks in the tail,

263
00:10:26,182 --> 00:10:28,777
to reduce drag and save fuel.

264
00:10:28,957 --> 00:10:31,005
But Concorde did it on a much

265
00:10:31,005 --> 00:10:34,255
bigger scale, because at supersonic speeds the

266
00:10:34,255 --> 00:10:35,667
change was so large.

267
00:10:35,847 --> 00:10:36,934
A simple idea,

268
00:10:36,934 --> 00:10:39,960
move the weight instead of fighting it,

269
00:10:39,960 --> 00:10:41,745
made Mach two possible.

270
00:10:42,095 --> 00:10:45,085
Why did Concorde have such tiny passenger

271
00:10:45,085 --> 00:10:47,564
windows, about the size of a hand?

272
00:10:47,744 --> 00:10:51,000
Because it flew at around sixty thousand

273
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,523
feet, almost twice as high as a

274
00:10:53,523 --> 00:10:54,826
normal airliner.

275
00:10:55,006 --> 00:10:57,175
Up there, if a window broke,

276
00:10:57,175 --> 00:10:59,731
the cabin would lose its air very

277
00:10:59,731 --> 00:11:02,210
fast, and the air outside is too

278
00:11:02,210 --> 00:11:04,689
thin to stay conscious for long.

279
00:11:04,869 --> 00:11:07,625
Small windows slow down the rate of

280
00:11:07,625 --> 00:11:10,697
pressure loss if the cabin is breached.

281
00:11:11,046 --> 00:11:12,397
From that height,

282
00:11:12,397 --> 00:11:15,338
passengers could see the curve of the

283
00:11:15,338 --> 00:11:17,801
Earth and a dark blue sky above

284
00:11:17,801 --> 00:11:18,198
them.

285
00:11:18,378 --> 00:11:21,413
The cabin was pressurized to about six

286
00:11:21,413 --> 00:11:24,926
thousand feet, similar to a normal airliner.

287
00:11:25,106 --> 00:11:27,328
The cabin itself was narrow,

288
00:11:27,328 --> 00:11:28,995
about nine feet wide,

289
00:11:28,995 --> 00:11:30,900
with four seats per row.

290
00:11:31,080 --> 00:11:33,101
Speed was the luxury,

291
00:11:33,101 --> 00:11:34,063
not space.

292
00:11:34,413 --> 00:11:37,841
Passengers also noticed that the windows felt

293
00:11:37,841 --> 00:11:38,222
warm.

294
00:11:38,402 --> 00:11:39,236
At Mach two,

295
00:11:39,236 --> 00:11:41,879
friction with the air heated the outer

296
00:11:41,879 --> 00:11:44,730
skin to around one hundred twenty degrees

297
00:11:44,730 --> 00:11:45,286
Celsius.

298
00:11:45,466 --> 00:11:47,475
Which brings us to the next part.

299
00:11:47,655 --> 00:11:49,918
That heat changed the shape of the

300
00:11:49,918 --> 00:11:50,917
whole airplane.

301
00:11:51,267 --> 00:11:54,843
Why was Concorde almost always painted white?

302
00:11:55,023 --> 00:11:57,345
Because white reflects heat.

303
00:11:57,525 --> 00:11:58,494
At Mach two,

304
00:11:58,494 --> 00:12:00,755
the skin got so hot that the

305
00:12:00,755 --> 00:12:02,935
aluminum airframe expanded,

306
00:12:02,935 --> 00:12:06,083
and Concorde grew between six and eight

307
00:12:06,083 --> 00:12:08,021
inches longer in flight.

308
00:12:08,201 --> 00:12:11,594
A special white paint kept the temperature

309
00:12:11,594 --> 00:12:13,130
as low as possible,

310
00:12:13,130 --> 00:12:16,523
because aluminum loses strength if it gets

311
00:12:16,523 --> 00:12:18,220
too hot for too long.

312
00:12:18,570 --> 00:12:20,671
In nineteen ninety-six,

313
00:12:20,671 --> 00:12:24,232
Air France painted one Concorde in dark

314
00:12:24,232 --> 00:12:26,697
blue for a Pepsi promotion.

315
00:12:26,877 --> 00:12:29,891
Because dark paint absorbs more heat,

316
00:12:29,891 --> 00:12:32,743
that Concorde was allowed to fly at

317
00:12:32,743 --> 00:12:35,349
Mach two for no more than twenty

318
00:12:35,349 --> 00:12:36,816
minutes at a time.

319
00:12:36,996 --> 00:12:38,972
Below Mach one point seven,

320
00:12:38,972 --> 00:12:40,362
there was no limit.

321
00:12:40,542 --> 00:12:42,572
It is one of the few airplanes

322
00:12:42,572 --> 00:12:45,210
where the paint color changed the speed

323
00:12:45,210 --> 00:12:45,616
limit.

324
00:12:45,965 --> 00:12:49,332
The expansion was visible inside too.

325
00:12:49,512 --> 00:12:52,262
Crews told stories of a gap opening

326
00:12:52,262 --> 00:12:55,639
near the flight engineer's panel at cruise,

327
00:12:55,639 --> 00:12:58,231
which closed again after landing.

328
00:12:58,411 --> 00:13:02,400
Concorde's engineers chose an aluminum alloy that

329
00:13:02,400 --> 00:13:05,494
could live with that heat for decades.

330
00:13:05,674 --> 00:13:06,654
At Mach three,

331
00:13:06,654 --> 00:13:09,245
the skin would have been around three

332
00:13:09,245 --> 00:13:10,786
hundred fifty degrees,

333
00:13:10,786 --> 00:13:12,256
too hot for aluminum.

334
00:13:12,436 --> 00:13:15,027
That is one reason Concorde stopped at

335
00:13:15,027 --> 00:13:15,641
Mach two.

336
00:13:15,990 --> 00:13:18,236
Why did Concorde have brakes made of

337
00:13:18,236 --> 00:13:18,672
carbon?

338
00:13:18,852 --> 00:13:21,116
Because it landed fast and heavy,

339
00:13:21,116 --> 00:13:23,585
and steel brakes would have been too

340
00:13:23,585 --> 00:13:24,820
heavy for the job.

341
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:28,157
Concorde was the first airliner with carbon

342
00:13:28,157 --> 00:13:30,213
brakes, developed by Dunlop.

343
00:13:30,393 --> 00:13:33,260
They saved about one thousand two hundred

344
00:13:33,260 --> 00:13:35,638
pounds compared with steel brakes,

345
00:13:35,638 --> 00:13:38,714
and they could survive temperatures of three

346
00:13:38,714 --> 00:13:41,861
hundred to four hundred degrees Celsius after

347
00:13:41,861 --> 00:13:42,421
landing.

348
00:13:42,770 --> 00:13:45,482
Carbon brakes are now standard on most

349
00:13:45,482 --> 00:13:49,050
large airliners, from the Boeing seven forty-seven

350
00:13:49,050 --> 00:13:51,048
to the Airbus A three fifty.

351
00:13:51,228 --> 00:13:53,184
Like many Concorde ideas,

352
00:13:53,184 --> 00:13:56,078
they were too early and too expensive

353
00:13:56,078 --> 00:13:58,659
at first, and then became normal.

354
00:13:58,839 --> 00:14:03,247
Fly-by-wire controls, digital engine computers,

355
00:14:03,247 --> 00:14:07,562
carbon brakes, Concorde tested them in airline

356
00:14:07,562 --> 00:14:10,751
service before almost anyone else.

357
00:14:11,100 --> 00:14:14,028
The wheels and tires also played a

358
00:14:14,028 --> 00:14:16,697
part in Concorde's darkest day.

359
00:14:16,877 --> 00:14:20,224
On the twenty-fifth of July two thousand,

360
00:14:20,224 --> 00:14:23,817
Air France flight forty-five ninety ran over

361
00:14:23,817 --> 00:14:26,267
a metal strip on the runway in

362
00:14:26,267 --> 00:14:26,757
Paris.

363
00:14:26,937 --> 00:14:28,115
A tire burst,

364
00:14:28,115 --> 00:14:30,473
debris struck a fuel tank,

365
00:14:30,473 --> 00:14:32,196
and a fire started.

366
00:14:32,376 --> 00:14:34,768
One hundred thirteen people died.

367
00:14:34,948 --> 00:14:38,983
Afterwards, Concorde got Kevlar linings inside its

368
00:14:38,983 --> 00:14:42,293
fuel tanks and new burst-resistant tires,

369
00:14:42,293 --> 00:14:45,118
and flew again in two thousand one.

370
00:14:45,467 --> 00:14:46,756
So here are the ten.

371
00:14:46,936 --> 00:14:49,110
A nose that bends to see the

372
00:14:49,110 --> 00:14:51,438
runway, and a visor to make it

373
00:14:51,438 --> 00:14:52,370
sharp again.

374
00:14:52,550 --> 00:14:55,289
A wing that lifts with swirls instead

375
00:14:55,289 --> 00:14:57,584
of flaps, and a wheel under the

376
00:14:57,584 --> 00:14:59,434
tail for the steep angle.

377
00:14:59,614 --> 00:15:01,643
Intakes that slow the air down,

378
00:15:01,643 --> 00:15:03,933
afterburners for the sound barrier,

379
00:15:03,933 --> 00:15:06,550
fuel that moves to balance the airplane,

380
00:15:06,550 --> 00:15:08,513
tiny windows for the altitude,

381
00:15:08,513 --> 00:15:10,149
white paint for the heat,

382
00:15:10,149 --> 00:15:12,374
and carbon brakes for the landing.

383
00:15:12,723 --> 00:15:16,648
Concorde was retired in two thousand three,

384
00:15:16,648 --> 00:15:20,299
and no passenger airplane has flown that

385
00:15:20,299 --> 00:15:21,303
fast since.

386
00:15:21,483 --> 00:15:24,110
Every strange part was the price of

387
00:15:24,110 --> 00:15:24,560
speed.

388
00:15:24,740 --> 00:15:27,788
If you want more machines explained part

389
00:15:27,788 --> 00:15:30,684
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

390
00:15:30,864 --> 00:15:32,947
And tell us in the comments which

